# Architecture

## Layer ownership

| Layer | Owns | Must not own |
|-------|------|--------------|
| Controller / `@Implement` | Wire HTTP/RPC → service | Business rules, DB queries |
| Service | Business logic, orchestration, typed results | Framework transport details |
| Repository / Prisma access | Persistence | Domain policy |
| Contract (`packages/contract`) | Public API shapes | Implementation |
| Client (`packages/client`) | Typed consumption | Business rules |
| DTO / types / enums / errors / constants | Definitions | Logic |

## NestJS app layout

Follow Caesar / Onion Pay: `AppModule` only composes.

```
apps/api/src/
  app.module.ts
  modules/
    index.ts                 # [...sharedModules, ...admin, ...user]
    user/                    # mobile / public product surface
    admin/                   # empty until catalog management exists
    shared-modules/          # Prisma, auth, mail, push, health, dispatch
  shared/                    # types, utils, constants, enums
```

Each domain is a Nest module with a thin oRPC (or HTTP) controller:

```
feature/
├── feature.controller.ts   # thin
├── feature.service.ts      # logic + Result types
├── feature.module.ts
├── dto/
├── types/
├── enums/
├── constants/
├── errors/
└── index.ts                # barrel at module boundary
```

Keep DTOs, types, enums, constants, errors, and decorators **outside** service files.

Do not import `modules/user` from `modules/shared-modules`. Shared-modules may be imported by user/admin.

## Service results

Expected failures return typed results — do not throw for normal business outcomes.

```typescript
type Result<T, E = DomainError> =
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; error: E };
```

## Dependency injection

Inject external dependencies. Do not `new` clients, Prisma, or SDKs inside services
except inside Nest providers/factories.

## Side effects

Emails, push, webhooks, and jobs are decoupled via events/queues/handlers — not
inline in the middle of domain mutations unless the operation is trivially local.
